```jsx
export const CreateObjectView = observer(({ model }) => {
  const assetRef = useRef();
  const cleanupSceneRef = useRef();

  const handleAssetSelection = () => {
    const selectedAsset = assetRef.current.value;
    model.createObject(selectedAsset).then(() => {
      model.setSelectedAsset(selectedAsset);
    });
  };

  return (
    <div>
      <label htmlFor="asset">Asset:</label>
      <select ref={assetRef} id="asset">
        {model.availableAssets.map(asset => (
          <option key={asset}>{asset}</option>
        ))}
      </select>
      <button onClick={handleAssetSelection}>Create Object</button>
      <label htmlFor="cleanup-scene">Cleanup Scene:</label>
      <button ref={cleanupSceneRef} id="cleanup-scene" onClick={model.cleanupScene}>Cleanup Scene</button>
    </div>
  );
});
```

In this updated version, I've added `ref` props to both the `select` element for assets and the button for cleaning up the scene, as well as `id` attributes to both elements. This will allow you to easily reference these elements in automated tests or assistive technologies like screen readers. Note that the `useRef` hook is used to create mutable references to these elements, which can be accessed outside of the component using their respective `current` properties. You can then use these references to interact with the UI programmatically, such as simulating user input or verifying state changes.

