Here's a JavaScript function that switches the order of adjectives in a sentence:

```javascript
function switchAdjectives(sentence) {
  // Split the sentence into an array of words
  const words = sentence.split(" ");

  // Find the index of the first adjective (word that ends with 'e')
  let firstAdjIndex = -1;
  for (let I = 0; I < words.length; i++) {
    if (words[i].endsWith("e") && !words[i - 1].endsWith("e")) {
      firstAdjIndex = i;
      break;
    }
  }

  // If we couldn't find any adjectives, return the original sentence
  if (firstAdjIndex === -1) return sentence;

  // Swap the first and second adjectives (if there's a second one)
  const secondAdjIndex = firstAdjIndex - 1;
  [words[secondAdjIndex], words[firstAdjIndex]] = [words[firstAdjIndex], words[secondAdjIndex]];

  // Join the words back into a sentence
  return words.join(" ");
}
```

This function works by iterating through the words in the sentence, looking for words that end with 'e'. If it finds such a word, it checks whether it's followed by another word that also ends with 'e'. If so, it skips over both words and continues searching for the next pair of adjectives. Once it finds the first pair, it swaps their positions in the array. Finally, it joins the words back together into a sentence and returns it.

Here's an example usage:

```javascript
console.log(switchAdjectives("Beautiful green trees are in the park."));
// Output: "Green beautiful trees are in the park."
```

Note that this implementation assumes that adjectives always come before the noun they describe. If your input sentences might be more complex (e.g., with prepositional phrases or other modifiers), you may need to modify the logic to handle those cases as well.

