Here are some steps you can follow to help Facebook pick up an image when sharing your WordPress blog:

1. Make sure you have an image in your blog post. You can add an image by clicking the "Add Block" button and selecting the "Image" block. Upload or select the image you want to use, then adjust its size and alignment as needed.

2. Check that the image is large enough. Facebook recommends using images that are at least 1200 x 630 pixels for best results. This will ensure that the image looks clear and sharp when it's displayed on different devices.

3. Add alt text to the image. Alt text is a short description of the image that helps people who are visually impaired understand what the image is about. It also helps search engines understand the content of the image, which can improve your SEO. To add alt text, click on the image in your blog post, then click the "Edit" link that appears in the top toolbar. Enter the alt text in the "Alternative Text" field, then click "Update".

4. Verify that the Yoast SEO plugin is installed and activated. This plugin adds meta tags to your blog post, including an Open Graph tag for the image. If you don't have Yoast SEO, you can add the Open Graph tag manually by adding the following code to your theme's functions.php file:

```php
function add_og_image() {
    global $post;
    if (is_single()) {
        echo '<meta property="og:image" content="' . Get_the_post_thumbnail_url($post->ID, 'large') . '" />';
    }
}
add_action('wp_head', 'add_og_image');
```

This code will add an Open Graph image tag to your blog post header, using the first image in the post as the default.

5. Test your blog post in the Facebook Sharing Debugger. Go to the Sharing Debugger, enter the URL of your blog post, and click "Debug". This will show you how Facebook sees your blog post, including any errors or warnings. You can also preview how the post will look when it's shared, which can help you identify any issues with the image or other elements.

