response:
To make this solution more efficient for handling large amounts of text, we can use memory-mapped files (mmaps) instead of loading the entire text into memory at once. Here's an updated implementation:

```matlab
function uniqueWords = findUniqueWords(text1File, text2File)
    % Read text1 and text2 into memory-mapped files
    mmapOptions = 'r';
    mmapSize = max(1024*1024*1024, 2*stat(text1File).Size); % Allocate enough space for 2GB of data
    mmaps = mmopen(text1File, mmapOptions, 'clobber', true, 'mmapSize', mmapSize);
    mmaps{2} = mmopen(text2File, mmapOptions, 'clobber', true, 'mmapSize', mmapSize);
    text1 = mmread(mmaps{1});
    text2 = mmread(mmaps{2});
    mmclose(mmaps);

    % Convert text to lowercase and split into words
    words1 = lower(strsplit(text1));
    words2 = lower(strsplit(text2));

    % Find unique words in text1 that do not appear in text2
    uniqueWords = setdiff(words1, words2);
end
```

The `mmopen()` function is used to open the text files as memory-mapped files, which allows us to access them in chunks without having to load the entire contents into memory. The `mmapSize` option specifies the maximum size of the mapped region, and we allocate enough space for 2GB of data to ensure we don't run out of memory for very large files. The `mmaps` variable is now a cell array containing two handles to the memory-mapped files. We read the contents of each file into a separate variable using `mmread()`, and then close the memory-mapped files using `mmclose()`. Note that because memory-mapped files are accessed in chunks, you may need to adjust the chunk size passed to `mmread()` depending on the characteristics of your data. For example, you might want to specify a smaller chunk size for text with many short lines, and a

