 To address the `InputMismatchException` in your Java program, you can add input validation to ensure that the user enters an integer. Here's how you can modify your `main()` method:

```java
public class RetrieveInputExample {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        Scanner scanner = new Scanner(System.in);

        System.out.print("Enter a number: ");

        int num = 0;

        while (true) {
            try {
                num = scanner.nextInt();
                break;
            } catch (InputMismatchException e) {
                System.out.println("Invalid input! Please enter an integer.");
                scanner.nextLine(); // Consume remaining characters on the line
            }
        }

        System.out.printf("The square of %d is %.2f", num, Math.pow(num, 2));

        scanner.close();
    }
}
```

In this modified version of the code, we've added a `while` loop with an inner `try-catch` block for handling the `InputMismatchException`. Inside the `catch` block, we print an error message and call `scanner.nextLine()` to consume any remaining characters on the current input line. This ensures that we don't accidentally process invalid input as part of our calculation.

Note that we also declared the `int` variable `num` outside the `while` loop to prevent it from being reset each time the loop executes. By doing so, we ensure that we only store the valid integer entered by the user in the `num` variable, regardless of how many times the user may need to retry entering a valid integer.

With these modifications, the program will now handle invalid input gracefully and prompt the user to reenter a valid integer until one is provided.

